.\" ai-tools-operator.conf(5) -- host configuration of Agent Tools Restricted.
.\" @AI_TOOLS_VERSION@ is substituted at deploy time (install.sh install_subst
.\" and the RPM %prep), like ai-tools(1). The grammar described here is the one
.\" conf.lib.sh implements for every KEY=value file in the project.
.TH AI-TOOLS-OPERATOR.CONF 5 "" "ai-tools @AI_TOOLS_VERSION@" "Agent Tools Restricted"
.SH NAME
ai-tools-operator.conf \- host options for Agent Tools Restricted
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /etc/ai-tools/operator.conf
.SH DESCRIPTION
Host-wide configuration for the sandboxed coding agent: which agents and
integrations a session receives, which command filters it applies, and which
directories the maintenance walks skip.
.PP
Every option is optional, and the shipped file sets only
.BR OPERATORS .
An absent key keeps its default, so a fresh install runs unedited and a file
written for an earlier version keeps working unchanged when a new option
appears. Each option states what its absence means, since the defaults
differ: an absent provider list enables the installed baseline, while an
absent strictness switch leaves the permissive posture in place.
.PP
Edit it as root; it is owned by
.B root
at mode
.BR 0644 .
An upgrade never overwrites it; see
.BR UPGRADES .
.SH GRAMMAR
One
.I KEY=VALUE
per line.
.TP
.I KEY=VALUE
Bare value; quotes are not required.
.TP
.BR KEY=\(dqa\ b\(dq ", " KEY=\(aqa\ b\(aq
One layer of matched quotes is stripped. Whitespace around the key and the
.B =
is trimmed.
.TP
.B KEY=a, b  c
List items separate on commas
.B and
whitespace, freely mixed; runs collapse and empty items are dropped.
.TP
.B KEY=[a, b]
The bracketed form of the same list, which the shipped template uses.
Inside the brackets items separate on commas and whitespace, so
.B [a,]
is the list
.BR a ,
and
.B []
is the empty list.
A list item does not contain whitespace, a comma, a bracket or a quote.
.TP
.B KEY=[a
.TQ
.B KEY=\(dq[a]\(dq
.TQ
.B KEY=[a,\ \(dqb\(dq]
Invalid: one bracket without the other, quotes around the brackets, or a quote
or a bracket inside them.
An invalid list reads as the
.B empty
list and is reported, never as an absent key, so for
.BR OPERATORS ,
.B AI_TOOLS_AGENTS
and
.B AI_TOOLS_INTEGRATIONS
it enrols or enables none.
.TP
.B KEY=value   # why
An inline comment. A
.B #
ends the value at the start of it or after whitespace; inside quotes it is
literal, so a value containing one is written
.BR KEY=\(dqa#b\(dq .
.TP
.B KEY=
Present with an
.B empty
value \(em distinct from an absent key. An option that reads a list uses this,
and
.BR KEY=[] ,
to mean "none", where an absent key means "the default".
.TP
.BR KEY=yes " (a switch)"
A switch reads
.BR yes ,
.BR true ,
.B 1
and
.B on
as yes, and
.BR no ,
.BR false ,
.BR 0 ,
.B off
and an empty value as no, in any case and quoted or not. Any other value reads
as no and is reported, so a mistyped switch does not change what a launch does
without a line saying so.
.PP
A line beginning with
.B #
is a comment. A repeated key takes its last assignment; a line with no
.B =
is ignored.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI OPERATORS= "USER\fR[,\fP USER\fR...]\fP"
The accounts enrolled as operators: the root helpers resolve each project's
owner from this list, and the
.BR ai-tools (1)
commands that act on a project require the caller to be listed.
Launching a session is gated by membership of the
.B ai-ops
group, which
.B "ai-tools-admin operators add"
grants together with the entry here; edit by hand only to review or remove.
.B Default:
no account.
.TP
.BI AI_TOOLS_AGENTS= "NAME\fR[,\fP NAME\fR...]\fP"
The coding agents a session may run, by provider name (a manifest basename under
.IR /usr/local/lib/ai-tools/agents.d ).
Exactly the names listed;
.B []
enables none.
.B Default:
no agent.
Every agent package ships disabled, and
.B "ai-tools-admin system bootstrap"
asks which one installed agent to enable and writes this line for it, or writes
the names given to its
.B \-\-agents
option; see
.BR ai-tools-admin (8).
.IP
Every agent named here runs as the one sandbox account and reads what the
others store: a login or a token one agent keeps, its session history, and
a custom endpoint token where one is configured, are readable by every session
of every agent named, and a session of one agent can start another named
agent's binary inside itself.
A second agent is therefore a line you write by hand, followed by a further
bootstrap; the bootstrap says the same once when it reads a line naming more
than one.
Names are reported at launch, so
.B "ai-tools providers"
shows the resolved set.
.TP
.BI AI_TOOLS_INTEGRATIONS= "NAME\fR[,\fP NAME\fR...]\fP"
Host-toolchain integrations a session receives, by provider name (a manifest
basename under
.IR /usr/local/lib/ai-tools/integrations.d ).
Exactly the names listed;
.B []
enables none.
.B Default:
each installed integration whose package ships it enabled
.RB ( default_enable=yes ).
An integration that widens host surface ships
.B default_enable=no
and is enabled only by naming it here \(em the dotnet integration, for example.
.TP
.BI AI_TOOLS_FILTERS= "NAME\fR[,\fP NAME\fR...]\fP"
The token-saving command-filter rule sets a session applies, by rule-set name (a
file basename under
.IR /usr/local/lib/ai-tools/filters.d ).
A filter rewrites a matching command so the tool prints less and strips terminal
control noise from what it printed.
Exactly the sets named, so
.B []
turns filtering off entirely.
.B Default:
every installed rule set applies.
Filtering never decides what the agent may run: the permission rules in the
agent's settings are evaluated on the rewritten command.
.TP
.B AI_TOOLS_REQUIRE_SELINUX=yes
SELinux strictness.
Where SELinux is enforcing and the
.B ai_tools
policy module is loaded, a session is confined at either setting, and an
entrypoint carrying the wrong label refuses the launch at either setting.
This key governs the two states the launch preflight reports as unverified:
SELinux not enforcing, and the module's file contexts not live.
.B Default
(key absent): such a launch proceeds without the SELinux layer, which is
the posture of an intentional DAC-only host; the launch log records
.BR require=no .
.B Set:
those two states refuse the launch instead
.RB ( require-not-enforcing ,
.BR require-inactive ),
so a host that expects confinement cannot start an unconfined session
unnoticed.
The key is read only while this file is root-owned and not group- or
other-writable, so a session cannot set or clear it.
On a host where SELinux is enforcing and the module is loaded,
.B sudo ai\-tools\-admin system bootstrap
offers to set this key and
.B AI_TOOLS_REQUIRE_ENTRYPOINT_VERIFY
while neither is present; the offer defaults to yes, a run with no terminal
takes it, and a declined offer writes
.BR no .
.TP
.B AI_TOOLS_REQUIRE_ENTRYPOINT_VERIFY=yes
Entrypoint-verification strictness. Each agent's entrypoint is verified against
the per-release checksum its vendor signed, and the verified value is recorded in
a root-owned pin under
.IR /var/opt/ai-tools/state/entrypoint-pin.d/ ;
the launch shim compares the entrypoint against that pin with no network of its
own. A
.B mismatch
refuses the launch at every setting \(em that is the tamper signal. This key
governs only the other outcome, an entrypoint carrying
.B no pin at all.
.B Default
(key absent): such a launch proceeds, because unpinned is also the state of a
host that has not yet run a relabel, one whose vendor published no manifest for
the installed release, and any air\-gapped host.
.B Set:
only a pinned entrypoint may start a session. A pin records either the checksum
the vendor signed for the installed release or, for an agent whose vendor
publishes no such manifest, the binary as installed \(em
.B ai\-tools status
names which of the two each agent holds, as
.B VERIFIED
or
.BR UNCHANGED .
Pin one with
.BR "sudo ai\-tools\-admin system entrypoints relabel" ,
which fetches the vendor's signed manifest where there is one and so needs the
host online.
.B sudo ai\-tools\-admin system bootstrap
offers this key on an enforcing host, as it does
.BR AI_TOOLS_REQUIRE_SELINUX ,
only while every enabled agent carries a pin.
.TP
.BI CLAUDE_SYSTEM_PROMPT_FILE= FILE
A custom system prompt for Claude Code sessions (requires the
.B ai-tools-agents-claude-code-restricted
agent). The launch wrapper reads
.I FILE
at launch and passes it to Claude Code as
.B --append-system-prompt-file
(or
.B --system-prompt-file\fR,\fP
per the mode).
.B Default
(key absent or empty): Claude Code's own default prompt is used.
.I FILE
must resolve under
.IR /etc/ai-tools/prompts/ ,
be root-owned and not group/other-writable, be readable by the
.B ai-tools
group, and hold plain text (the shipped default is 0640 root:ai-tools; the
operator need not read it, since the wrapper only stats the file and the
session checks that it is text before it starts) \(em that
directory is the one location the confined session is granted read on. Its
.B full contents are sent to the model verbatim
(no markup is stripped), so it must contain only prompt text. A
configured prompt that cannot be applied
.B refuses
the launch rather than silently reverting to the default, so a broken value fails
closed. The shipped default file is
.B empty;
write your prompt into it, or keep your own prompt files under
.I /etc/ai-tools/prompts/
(root-owned, not group/other-writable) and point this key at the one you want.
The default ships
.B 0640 root:ai-tools
so a custom prompt is not world-readable; the sandbox account reads it and you
edit it with
.BR sudo .
.TP
.BR CLAUDE_SYSTEM_PROMPT_MODE=append | replace
How
.B CLAUDE_SYSTEM_PROMPT_FILE
is applied.
.B append
layers the file after Claude Code's default prompt, keeping its
built-in tool-use and safety guidance;
.B replace
drops the default entirely \(em restate whatever tool-use/safety guidance the
session still needs. Reach for
.B replace
when
.B ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL
points at a non-Anthropic-compatible endpoint whose model does not share Claude
Code's assumptions about itself. Note that
.B replace
sets only the request's system prompt: Claude Code still sends its tool
definitions and the
.I CLAUDE.md
context (collected from the working directory and its parents) in separate request
fields, so it does not reduce what the model receives to only this file \(em for a
non-Anthropic endpoint, shape the final request at the proxy.
.B Default:
.BR append .
.IP
In either mode the shipped orientation text,
.IR /opt/ai-tools/orientation/AGENTS.md
(linked into the agent's configuration directory under the name it reads as
user-scope instructions), still loads. It arrives as memory context in the
conversation, not in the system prompt, so it states what the sandbox refuses
beneath whatever this file says. A system-prompt instruction outweighs memory
context, so a prompt that contradicts the orientation wins; write the prompt so
the two agree.
.TP
.BI CLAUDE_BASE_URL_FILE= FILE
A dedicated file routing Claude Code sessions at a custom API endpoint (requires
the
.B ai-tools-agents-claude-code-restricted
agent).
.B Default
(key absent or empty): the default Anthropic endpoint is used.
.I FILE
must resolve under
.IR /etc/ai-tools/endpoints/ ,
be root-owned and not group/other-writable. The file is parsed with this same
grammar and only four keys are read from it \(em
.BR ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL " (required, an http(s) URL),"
.BR ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN ", " ANTHROPIC_MODEL ", and " ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_HAIKU_MODEL .
The token belongs in that file, which ships
.B 640 root:ai-tools
(not world-readable), never in this file. Each set option is validated at
launch and a set option that is invalid
.B refuses
the launch. The file's options, their validation and their precedence
against a Claude Code settings
.B env
block are in
.BR custom\-claude\-endpoint.conf (5).
.TP
.BI SKIP_VCS_DIRS= "NAME\fR...\fP"
.TQ
.BI SKIP_PACKAGE_DIRS= "NAME\fR...\fP"
.TQ
.BI SKIP_ARTIFACT_DIRS= "NAME\fR...\fP"
.TQ
.BI SKIP_CACHE_DIRS= "NAME\fR...\fP"
Directory
.B names
(not paths) omitted from the helper walks \(em ownership hand-back sweeps, claim
normalization, and secret lockdown. Setting a key
.B replaces
that category's default rather than adding to it, and
.B []
skips none of that category.
Skipping bounds how much of a tree those
walks traverse. It is a cost setting: the agent reaches a skipped directory
through the ordinary filesystem permissions, exactly as it reaches any other.
.B Defaults:
.IP
.EX
SKIP_VCS_DIRS=[.git]
SKIP_PACKAGE_DIRS=[node_modules, .venv, packages]
SKIP_ARTIFACT_DIRS=[]
SKIP_CACHE_DIRS=[__pycache__]
.EE
.TP
.BI SKIP_ARTIFACT_DIRS_EXCLUDED_PATHS_RELATIVE= "DIRECTORY\fR...\fP"
Project-relative paths that keep being walked even though their basename matches
.BR SKIP_ARTIFACT_DIRS .
For a source directory that shares a build-output name, such as a
.I bin
directory holding scripts rather than compiled output.
A list item does not carry whitespace, so a path containing a space cannot be
listed here.
.B Default:
.BR [] .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/ai-tools/operator.conf
This file.
.TP
.I /usr/local/lib/ai-tools/agents.d/
.TQ
.I /usr/local/lib/ai-tools/integrations.d/
.TQ
.I /usr/local/lib/ai-tools/filters.d/
Provider manifests and rule sets, keyed by the provider names.
.SH NOTES
.SS Upgrades
This file ships as
.BR %config(noreplace) ,
so an upgrade never overwrites what you wrote. When the shipped file has
changed, rpm keeps yours and writes the new one alongside as
.IR /etc/ai\-tools/operator.conf.rpmnew .
.PP
Merging the two is left to you. An option block is prose, and an automatic
merge of prose would need a convention you would have to learn before you
could predict what an upgrade does to this file.
.PP
There is no hurry. Every option is optional and an unmentioned key keeps its
default, so a file written before an option existed keeps working exactly as
it did; what a stale file costs is knowledge of the option, not behaviour.
.PP
To list the options the new version documents that your file does not mention
and see the full difference:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
sudo ai\-tools\-admin system post\-upgrade
.EE
.RE
.PP
Against
.I operator.conf
that command only reports: it names the new options and shows the difference,
and every edit stays yours.
It leaves the
.I .rpmnew
in place as the copy you merge from, and names it so you can delete it once you
are done.
.SH EXAMPLES
Enable the dotnet integration and keep every default elsewhere:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
AI_TOOLS_INTEGRATIONS=[dotnet]
.EE
.RE
.PP
Turn command filtering off while diagnosing unexpected command output:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
AI_TOOLS_FILTERS=[]
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ai\-tools (1),
.BR allowed\-projects (5),
.BR custom\-claude\-endpoint.conf (5),
.BR secret\-patterns (5)
.PP
Report what a session actually resolves \(em the enabled agents and
integrations, and why \(em with
.BR "ai-tools providers" .
